{
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "taste_quality",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.9,
      "source_groundedness": 0.78,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Directly targets the query about distinct aspects of food quality; taste is a core axis and the descriptor values (excellent/bland/artificial/bitter) give real grouping leverage. Groundedness is mostly sound: 'good', 'excellent', 'artificial' (medicinal flavor) are well-read. However the peanut row is labeled 'poor' when the complaint is mislabeling/sizing, not taste, so the value overreaches the source somewhat."
    },
    {
      "name": "convenience_usability",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.75,
      "source_groundedness": 0.7,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Convenience/usability is a legitimate distinct aspect and adds a new axis beyond taste, useful for the query. Silent rows correctly become NULL. But the first sample text says nothing about ease of use yet is labeled 'straightforward', a confident specific the text does not support; the large 'straightforward'/'very_easy' counts raise concern that this label is often inferred without evidence."
    },
    {
      "name": "comparative_positioning",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.85,
      "source_groundedness": 0.6,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Comparative framing (superior/inferior/preferred) is a meaningful, distinct analytical axis. However groundedness is weak in samples: the dog food text does not compare to competitors yet is 'preferred'; the taffy text has no comparison yet 'preferred'; the confection labeled 'unique_unavailable' is not warranted. Values frequently assert positioning the source is silent on."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.85,
  "source_groundedness": 0.68,
  "rationale": "The three columns each provide a genuinely distinct aspect of food-quality reviews (taste, convenience, comparative positioning), which is exactly what the query asks for; none is near-constant, redundant, or a trivial length/sentiment flag. On groundedness, taste_quality is largely well-read, but convenience_usability and comparative_positioning both show a tendency to assign confident specific labels ('straightforward', 'preferred') to rows whose source text does not mention convenience or comparison, indicating over-inference rather than principled NULLs. Overall useful but with moderate grounding concerns."
}

